// IdentityServiceHandler is an implementation of the chronos.identity.v1.IdentityService service.
type IdentityServiceHandler interface {
	// Register claims an address and creates the account that claims it.
	//
	// Public: the caller has no session and, by definition, no account. It is a
	// WRITE rather than a GROW — a person is not a seat, and seats are consumed
	// when an organization adds a member, not when a human being signs up.
	//
	// It sets NO PASSWORD and creates no credential of any kind. The account is
	// unusable by everybody until the link mailed to the address is followed and
	// VerifyEmail supplies one.
	//
	// The response is identical whether or not the address was free. See
	// RegisterResponse.
	Register(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RegisterRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RegisterResponse], error)
	// VerifyEmail proves control of an address, sets the account's first
	// password, and makes the claim on the address permanent.
	//
	// Public: it is reached from a link in a mailbox, by a browser that has never
	// authenticated. The token IS the authentication, and it is single-use.
	//
	// It is the ONLY call that can give a brand-new account a password, and that
	// is the pre-hijacking defence: the credential is created by the party that
	// has just proven the mailbox, in the same request as the proof.
	VerifyEmail(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.VerifyEmailRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.VerifyEmailResponse], error)
	// ResendEmailVerification issues a new verification link for an address whose
	// account has not proven it yet.
	//
	// Public, and the choice is forced rather than preferred. A Pending account
	// can hold no session, so requiring one would make this call unavailable to
	// every account that needs it — the endpoint would exist and serve nobody.
	//
	// The cost of being public is that it is an unauthenticated mail trigger
	// pointed at a third party's mailbox, so it is rate limited on TWO axes before
	// anything is looked up: per address, which bounds mail-bombing one victim, and
	// per caller, which bounds an enumeration sweep. Both counters are consumed
	// whether or not an account exists, so the ceiling itself is not an oracle.
	//
	// A WRITE: the successful path appends EmailVerificationRequested to the
	// account's stream, and the verification reactor does the rest.
	ResendEmailVerification(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ResendEmailVerificationRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ResendEmailVerificationResponse], error)
	// CheckUsernameAvailability reports whether a public handle can be claimed.
	//
	// Public: it is reached from the signup form, by a browser that has never
	// authenticated and by definition has no account.
	//
	// # It IS an enumeration oracle, and that is the intended behaviour
	//
	// A caller can learn that @alice is taken. That is not a leak, because a handle
	// is PUBLISHED — publication is its entire purpose (ADR-051) — and the same
	// fact is readable from any page that renders a mention, from a profile URL,
	// and from the person themselves. Every other public RPC in this service is
	// shaped to make its two outcomes indistinguishable; this one is shaped to make
	// them distinguishable, and the difference is not an oversight in the schema.
	//
	// Do NOT "fix" this into an undifferentiated response. The resulting endpoint
	// would answer no question at all, the signup form could not tell a person
	// their handle was taken until after they had spent a verification link they
	// cannot get back, and the information would remain freely available everywhere
	// else in the product.
	//
	// The ONE thing it must not distinguish is a tombstoned handle from a taken
	// one; see CheckUsernameAvailabilityResponse for why that single merge is a
	// privacy control rather than the same tidiness.
	//
	// # The answer is advisory
	//
	// It reads the handle's reservation stream at an instant. Uniqueness is
	// enforced at the moment of the WRITE by an atomic append with a precondition
	// (ADR-044, ADR-051), so a handle reported free may be taken by the time
	// VerifyEmail claims it — any check-then-claim is racy by construction. The
	// point of this call is that a person finds out at the FORM rather than after
	// clicking a link.
	//
	// A READ: it appends nothing, mints nothing and sends no mail. It is rate
	// limited per caller regardless — "an attacker may learn whether @alice exists"
	// is not the same permission as "an attacker may read a stream from the event
	// store as fast as they can open sockets".
	CheckUsernameAvailability(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.CheckUsernameAvailabilityRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.CheckUsernameAvailabilityResponse], error)
	// RequestPasswordReset sends a reset link to the address a registered account
	// holds.
	//
	// Public, and the choice is forced rather than preferred: the population that
	// needs this call is exactly the population that cannot sign in.
	//
	// The cost of being public is that it is an unauthenticated mail trigger
	// pointed at a third party's mailbox, so it is rate limited on TWO axes before
	// anything is looked up — per address, which bounds mail-bombing one victim,
	// and per caller, which bounds an enumeration sweep. Both counters are
	// consumed whether or not an account exists, so the ceiling itself is not an
	// oracle. The per-address counter is the SAME one verification mail spends
	// (NOTIFICATIONS.md §4: "an hourly ceiling per address across all classes"), so
	// an attacker cannot double a victim's mail by alternating between the two
	// endpoints.
	//
	// A WRITE: the successful path appends PasswordResetRequested to the account's
	// stream, and the reset-mail issuer does the rest.
	RequestPasswordReset(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RequestPasswordResetRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RequestPasswordResetResponse], error)
	// ResetPassword redeems a reset link and replaces the account's password.
	//
	// Public: it is reached from a link in a mailbox, by a browser that has never
	// authenticated. The token IS the authentication, and it is single-use.
	//
	// It grants NOTHING beyond the credential change. No session is created, no
	// bearer token is returned, the second factor is untouched, and a Pending
	// account is not activated — so a reset cannot be used to bypass the second
	// factor (ASVS 5.0 V6.4.3). The caller's next act is an ordinary
	// CreateSession.
	//
	// In the same request it voids every session for the account including any
	// held by whoever is resetting, every outstanding token of every purpose, and
	// any pending identifier change (identity.md §4.4, §4.5).
	ResetPassword(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ResetPasswordRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ResetPasswordResponse], error)
	// Authenticate verifies the factors presented and reports what remains
	// (ADR-050).
	//
	// Public: this is where a session comes from, so requiring one is circular.
	//
	// A WRITE, and not a read despite minting nothing: every attempt appends to
	// the authentication journal, and classing it as a read would let it through
	// gates whose whole purpose is to stop writes. There is no `min_aal` for the
	// same reason there is no authz — the caller has no session to have a level.
	Authenticate(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.AuthenticateRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.AuthenticateResponse], error)
	// CreateSession completes a login and mints the bearer token.
	//
	// Public, for the same reason as Authenticate: it is the call that creates the
	// session everything else requires.
	CreateSession(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.CreateSessionRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.CreateSessionResponse], error)
	// GetUser returns the caller's own account: what it is, where it is in its
	// lifecycle, and whether its address has been proven.
	//
	// AAL1 — no `min_aal` declared, so the default applies. This discloses nothing
	// an attacker holding a session does not already have, and demanding step-up
	// to render the account screen would make step-up routine, which is how people
	// learn to approve it without reading it.
	GetUser(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.GetUserRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.GetUserResponse], error)
	// ListSessions returns the caller's device list, newest first, one page at a
	// time.
	//
	// AAL1. It is a read of a list the holder needs in order to NOTICE a
	// compromise, and putting step-up in front of noticing is backwards — the
	// revocation that follows is what carries the AAL2 requirement.
	ListSessions(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ListSessionsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ListSessionsResponse], error)
	// ListMethods returns the authentication methods on the caller's account:
	// which exist, what kind each is, whether it can be used now, and when it last
	// was.
	//
	// AAL1, on the same argument as ListSessions: this is the screen on which
	// somebody discovers an authenticator they did not enrol.
	ListMethods(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ListMethodsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ListMethodsResponse], error)
	// ListLoginHistory returns recent authentication attempts against the caller's
	// account, successes and failures alike, newest first.
	//
	// AAL1, and deliberately so even though this is the most sensitive read here.
	// It is the account holder's own security log, and it is only useful if they
	// actually look at it.
	ListLoginHistory(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ListLoginHistoryRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ListLoginHistoryResponse], error)
	// EnrollTotp provisions a new authenticator secret and returns it once.
	//
	// AAL2. Enrolling a second factor is how an attacker holding one factor makes
	// their access durable, so it must not be reachable from a session that only
	// ever presented one. It replaces any unproven enrolment on the account, so
	// the abandoned secret stops existing at the moment the new one is written
	// rather than lingering as a second confirmable factor.
	//
	// AAL1 while the account has never held a proven second factor, and only
	// then. Read the two levels together and they say: presenting the existing
	// factor is what buys the right to add another, and an account with no
	// existing factor is exempt from a demand nothing could satisfy. Without the
	// exemption an account registered through this API could never activate — the
	// factor it must have to reach AAL2 is the factor this call exists to give it
	// (identity.md §2).
	//
	// The exemption is not a weaker version of the same permission. It applies to
	// a strictly poorer target: an account with no second factor, whose password
	// is therefore already the only thing standing between an attacker and it. An
	// attacker who enrols there has gained nothing they did not already have. On
	// an account that HAS a factor the floor is AAL2, so the same attacker must
	// present that factor — and it does not re-open when a factor is removed,
	// because the window closes on first proof and never re-opens.
	EnrollTotp(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.EnrollTotpRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.EnrollTotpResponse], error)
	// ConfirmTotp proves possession of a provisioned secret, and may be what
	// activates the account.
	//
	// AAL2, matching EnrollTotp: a confirmation is the half of an enrolment that
	// makes a factor real, and letting it run at a lower level than the enrolment
	// itself would leave the pair only as strong as its weaker call.
	//
	// The bootstrap floor matches EnrollTotp for the same reason. The two calls
	// are one operation split in half, and exempting only the first would leave
	// an account able to provision a secret it could never prove — the deadlock
	// moved one call to the right rather than removed.
	//
	// This is the call that CLOSES the window: the account it activates has a
	// proven second factor from that moment, so the next call to either method
	// faces AAL2. The exemption is therefore self-limiting, and it is limited by
	// the very thing it was granted to produce.
	ConfirmTotp(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ConfirmTotpRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ConfirmTotpResponse], error)
	// GenerateRecoveryCodes replaces the whole recovery-code set and returns the
	// new plaintext codes once.
	//
	// AAL2. A recovery code is a standing bypass of every other factor, so minting
	// a fresh set from a single-factor session would hand an attacker a permanent
	// credential that survives the password change the victim makes in response.
	// Generating codes never activates an account — only a real second factor
	// does.
	//
	// NO bootstrap exemption, deliberately, and this is the method where the
	// decision is load-bearing. The exemption exists to break a deadlock, and
	// there is no deadlock here: recovery codes cannot activate an account, so
	// nothing about the path from registered to Active runs through this call. An
	// account reaches its first AAL2 session by confirming a TOTP secret, and only
	// then asks for codes — by which time the strict floor is satisfiable. Granting
	// the exemption anyway would mean a single-factor session could mint the one
	// credential that outlives every remedy the account holder has, on an account
	// that has no second factor to lose. That is the exact hole the floor is
	// shaped to keep shut, and it would be opened for no gain.
	GenerateRecoveryCodes(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.GenerateRecoveryCodesRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.GenerateRecoveryCodesResponse], error)
	// RevokeSession ends one of the caller's sessions.
	//
	// AAL2. Revocation is the control a person reaches for when they believe they
	// are compromised, and it must not be usable BY the compromise: a
	// single-factor session that could end sessions could sign the legitimate
	// holder out of the device they are using to investigate.
	RevokeSession(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RevokeSessionRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RevokeSessionResponse], error)
	// RevokeAllSessions ends every live session on the caller's account, in one
	// atomic append.
	//
	// AAL2, on the same argument as RevokeSession and more so: this is the
	// account-wide version, so the denial of service an attacker could inflict
	// with it is total.
	RevokeAllSessions(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RevokeAllSessionsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RevokeAllSessionsResponse], error)
	// DeactivateAccount switches the caller's own account off, and ends every
	// session on it in the same atomic append.
	//
	// # Reversible, and how
	//
	// Deactivation is the holder's own switch and the holder undoes it by SIGNING
	// IN again with every factor the account has — the successful authentication
	// records the reactivation in the same append as itself, so the account is on
	// again before the new session exists. There is deliberately no
	// `ReactivateAccount` RPC: it would need a session, a session needs an
	// authentication, and an authentication refuses a deactivated account, so its
	// only precondition would be one its own subject can never satisfy. See
	// `identity.md` §1.
	//
	// Suspension is the administrative counterpart and is NOT here. It is not
	// reversible by the holder, every method on this service is reached by the
	// holder acting on their own account, and there is no operator surface in this
	// repository yet — so an RPC for it could only be a self-suspension, which
	// makes the account unreachable by every route this module has.
	//
	// # AAL2
	//
	// On RevokeAllSessions' argument: the sign-out is total, so the denial of
	// service an attacker holding a stolen password could inflict with it is total.
	// No `bootstrap_min_aal`, deliberately — an account still enrolling its first
	// factor has nothing to switch off, and the exemption exists for enrolment
	// alone.
	DeactivateAccount(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.DeactivateAccountRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.DeactivateAccountResponse], error)
	// RequestAccountDeletion asks for the caller's own account to be erased, and
	// starts the grace period.
	//
	// # Nothing acts on it yet
	//
	// The request is appended to the account's stream and that is the whole
	// behaviour. Erasure is the compliance domain's work — destroy the key, and the
	// personal data becomes unreadable everywhere at once — and that module does not
	// exist. Neither does the workflow that runs the grace period, the mail that
	// names the date, nor a command to cancel. The account keeps working.
	//
	// This is stated on the RPC rather than only in a design document because a
	// client that reads "deletion requested" as "account gone" will build a screen
	// that lies to the person using it.
	//
	// # AAL2, plus a typed confirmation
	//
	// identity.md §2 lists account deletion among the operations that require
	// step-up. The step-up proves WHO is asking; the `confirmation` field is what
	// makes it hard to ask by accident, which matters here and nowhere else in this
	// service, because nothing in this module undoes it.
	//
	// # It does not sign anybody out
	//
	// Every other destructive call here voids sessions. This one deliberately does
	// not: the grace period exists so the person can change their mind, and signing
	// them out of an account that still works teaches them the request took effect
	// immediately, which it did not.
	RequestAccountDeletion(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RequestAccountDeletionRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RequestAccountDeletionResponse], error)
}

